# Erdős Problem #99 — Target A continuation prompt after five-claim audit

Work entirely in one context. Do not spawn delegated research agents. Internet literature
search is allowed when it materially helps, but verify exact hypotheses before importing a
result.

The objective is a Level I proof or disproof of Erdős Problem #99. The immediate priority is
Target A. Do not spend the run re-proving the trusted theorem stack.

## Canonical files

Read the current map in `../README.md`, then the shared theory and **all five** Target A route
modules indexed by `../targets/target-a/README.md`. In particular, read:

- `../targets/target-a/04-post-v1.7-audited-addenda.md`;
- `../targets/target-a/05-fixed-shape-and-separator-audit.md`.

Reports, older prompts, and the archived monolithic report are provenance rather than
independent theorem sources.

## Exact Target A endpoint

For the plateau-terminalized boundary response,
\[
\zeta=\dim\ker S=|C^\partial|-\operatorname{rank}S,
\qquad
s=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm int}.
\]
A hypothetical survivor has
\[
g=\Delta_\Lambda-d_{\rm sc}=s-\zeta\ge1.
\]
Target A closes exactly if
\[
\boxed{\zeta\ge s,}
\]
equivalently
\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{rank}S
\le d_{\rm int}+|C^\partial|-\Delta_\Lambda.
}
\]
The response is an exact planar strict gammoid. Planarity by itself does not force the
required rank loss.

## Binding fixed-hull correction

For the genuine source \(A\subset H\),
\[
\widehat p_H=\alpha_1(H)-|A|,
\]
and every unit-separated finite \(Q\subset H\) satisfies
\[
|Q|-|N_A(Q)|\le\widehat p_H.
\]
After maximal triangle-free extension \(B\), the analogous bound is
\[
|Q|-|N_B(Q)|\le\tau,
\qquad
\tau=\alpha_1(H)-|B|.
\]

Therefore do **not** seek a same-hull Hall witness of deficiency
\(\widehat p_H+1\), \(p+1\), or \(\tau+1\). Such a witness is impossible. A source
replacement is useful only if it yields a strict smaller-diameter competitor or is transported
directly into the Schur response.

The source-capacity exchange flag is Hall-tight and consumes the available fixed-hull slack
exactly.

## First-transition status

The exact fixed-container cone theorem gives this alternative at the first triangle-producing
flag member:

1. a simultaneous feasible motion breaks every unit triangle and yields a triangle-free global
   optimum at the same cardinality; or
2. some unit triangle is universally first-order locked by the active contact/container cone.

For a one-point slice, use the **closed** semicircle criterion. If the unit-contact directions
fit in a closed semicircle, a nontrivial finite motion exists; first-order-neutral contacts open
quadratically. Fixed-neighbor local isolation occurs only when no closed semicircle contains the
contact directions.

The exact two-leaf family in Section 139.1 is a binding regression: either leaf order creates a
locally isolated unit triangle. One-point leaf perturbation cannot close the tree branch.

The finite transition scan has been corrected accordingly. Do not use the older open-semicircle
caged counts.

## Cage-to-Schur transport remains global

Section 149 gives a formal local no-go. An exact source-side cage can coexist, at the level of
local hypotheses, with a planar strict-conflict response whose generic response matrix is
diagonal and full rank. Thus
\[
\text{local cage}+\text{planarity}+\text{generic response algebra}
\not\Rightarrow
\ker S\ne0.
\]

This is not a counterexample to Target A because the two objects are not globally coupled by one
minimizer. It proves the missing theorem must use the actual source/plateau/scaffold relation.
Do not claim that one locked triangle automatically supplies one Schur separator.

## Homothetic strain is closed for every fixed shape and fixed accumulated strain

Let \(K\) be any fixed full-dimensional convex triangular-lattice polygon with
\(0\in\operatorname{int}K\), and put
\[
P_L=\Lambda\cap LK.
\]
For every fixed \(\alpha>0\), Section 148 proves that the hull of
\[
\left(1+\frac\alpha L\right)P_L
\]
contains a unit-separated set with one additional point for all sufficiently large \(L\).

The proof moves the two lattice half-patches apart inside the homothetic margin, obtaining a
fixed positive linear gap, then inserts one finite Kuperberg–Kuperberg–Kuperberg witness.

This eliminates the free fixed-shape \((1+\alpha/L)\) regression. It does **not** yet handle
exterior blockers of an actual embedded source/scaffold monomer domain.

## Quantitative Kuperberg status

Two different complexity quantities must not be confused.

Let \(\kappa_*(d)\) be the minimum number of old centers deleted by any positive-gain finite
rearrangement of any width-\(d\) triangular gap packing. Section 146 proves
\[
\boxed{
\kappa_*(d)\to\infty
\qquad(d\downarrow0).
}
\]
The proof is a compactness argument against complete saturation of the perfect triangular
lattice. This is a genuine quantitative asymptotic lower-complexity theorem, but it gives no
explicit growth rate.

Separately let \(W(d)\) be the least support radius of a positive-gain finite witness. The
Kuperberg theorem gives only
\[
W(d)<\infty\qquad(d>0).
\]
No audited polynomial, exponential, or other explicit upper rate for \(W(d)\) is known from
the checked paper/literature.

For vanishing accumulated strain \(\alpha_L\to0\) in a fixed shape with
\(r\mathbb B\subset K\), the Section 150 embedding argument gives the sufficient condition
\[
W(r\alpha_L)=o(L).
\]
Do not invent a rate. Proving a usable support-radius rate is a legitimate new theorem only if
it connects to the actual scaffold scale.

## Highest-value next attacks

Prioritize one exact bridge:

1. **Exterior-compatible gap concentration.** Execute the fixed-shape row-split/Kuperberg
   replacement inside a genuinely derived monomer region while respecting all exterior
   blockers and obtain a strict diameter certificate.
2. **Locked-cage to Schur transport.** Use global first-transition/source data to turn locked
   triangles into at least \(s\) independent failures of the boundary alternating linkage.
3. **Capacity-shadow transport.** Push the bounded-displacement source shadow through plateau
   terminalization to an \(s\)-dimensional response kernel.
4. **Activation-rank compensation.** Force response dependence from the large independent
   activation-edge forest of the source.
5. **Quantitative gap support.** Prove an explicit upper bound for \(W(d)\) strong enough for a
   vanishing-gap regime that actually occurs in the canonical geometry.

Regression-test every proposed theorem against the exact two-leaf cage, corrected
closed-semicircle boundary cases, cyclic Hall circuits, distributed strain with exterior
locking, full-rank planar response examples, overlapping circuits, annular full-linkage
networks, near-equilateral Delaunay wells, regular shells, and caged branch junctions.

Do not call Target A solved until the exact Schur inequality is proved or a genuine global
source replacement gives a strictly smaller-diameter competitor.

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN. TARGET A REMAINS UNRESOLVED.**
